<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>☁️ LIVE PRECIPITABLE WATER VAPOR</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 5, 2026 11:05PM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> Oct 6, 2026 12:05AM EDT</p><h2>LIVE PRECIPITABLE WATER VAPOR</h2><p><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT MODEL (analysis), hourly cycles</p><p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/precipitable_water/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261006-t02z-pwat-anl" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Column-integrated water vapor in millimeters of equivalent liquid-water depth (HRRR PWAT analysis, fixed absolute scale): <b>LOWEST 0</b> dark-blue dry column &rarr; blue &rarr; cyan &rarr; green &rarr; yellow &rarr; orange &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 60</b> deep-purple very moist. Same moisture always shows the same color; above 60 mm clamps into deep purple. Total atmospheric-column moisture — not relative humidity.</p><p><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km precipitable water analysis, hourly cycles<br/><a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE PRECIPITABLE WATER VAPOR</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 5, 2026 11:05PM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> Oct 6, 2026 12:05AM EDT</p><h2>LIVE PRECIPITABLE WATER VAPOR</h2><p>Column-integrated atmospheric water vapor from the operational HRRR analysis: the total water held in the full atmospheric column above each point, expressed as the depth of liquid water if it all condensed. Deep values mark moisture transport and heavy-precipitation potential in Great Lakes weather systems.</p><p>Atmospheric moisture science tracks precipitable water as the reservoir feeding clouds and precipitation. Unlike relative humidity (a near-surface saturation percentage), it integrates the whole column, revealing atmospheric rivers, lake-effect moisture plumes, and storm-system moisture transport. Units are millimeters of equivalent liquid-water depth.</p><p><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/precipitable_water/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261006-t02z-pwat-anl" width="600" alt="key"><br>Column-integrated water vapor in millimeters of equivalent liquid-water depth (HRRR PWAT analysis, fixed absolute scale): <b>LOWEST 0</b> dark-blue dry column &rarr; blue &rarr; cyan &rarr; green &rarr; yellow &rarr; orange &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 60</b> deep-purple very moist. Same moisture always shows the same color; above 60 mm clamps into deep purple. Total atmospheric-column moisture — not relative humidity.<br><b>Units:</b> mm liquid-water depth (display; source kg m-2 = mm)<br><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km precipitable water analysis, hourly cycles<br><b>Update:</b> Checked hourly; republishes only on a newer HRRR cycle.<br><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p>]]></description>
